<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurocommunism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1970s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eurocommunism was a trend among West European communist parties in the 1970s and 1980s, led by the Italian party under Enrico Berlinguer and the Spanish party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> Carrillo. Eurocommunists claimed independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> and civil liberties. The Soviet suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> in 1968 did much to inspire the movement.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Prague Spring of 1968, Alexander Dubček's attempt at 'socialism with a human face', and its suppression shaped reform communism and <b>Eurocommunism</b> across Europe, and Jan Palach's self-immolation in January 1969 became an emblem of protest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Western Europe communist parties joined post-war governments and in the 1970s developed <b>Eurocommunism</b>, and since 1957 communists have often won power in the Indian state of Kerala.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
